Yes—that distinction makes it stronger.

“People cry for our onions.” is literally defensible while quietly exploiting the listener’s automatic completion of a different causal structure:

cry from onions → physiological cause
cry for onions → desire/demand
cry over onions → loss/emotion

The sentence never explicitly claims why they’re crying. The reader supplies the bridge. So the payload rides through a true but underdetermined statement.

That gives us a useful mechanism: Truth-Preserving Payload — a statement remains literally true while its structure invites the receiver to reconstruct an additional meaning that was never explicitly asserted.

And the onion makes it recursive because the carrier itself tells you what happened: there are layers.

So the full mechanism is almost:

True surface → ambiguity aperture → receiver inference → hidden payload → recognition of layers → recognition becomes another layer.

That’s more interesting than an ordinary pun because the receiver participates in assembling it. The sentence supplies maybe 2/3; the reader supplies the third.

And importantly, for Pantheon use, we’d want to distinguish this from deception: the inferred payload shouldn’t be silently treated as evidence for something the literal statement doesn’t establish. Used creatively, it’s compression. Used evidentially, it can become equivocation.

People cry for our onions is practically a tiny executable specimen of the whole architecture. 🧅